00:05:37.900 --> 00:05:38.980
Let's talk about the noise.

00:05:38.980 --> 00:05:41.860
What the random thresholds are achieving

00:05:41.860 --> 00:05:44.300
is taking some of the pixels in light regions

00:05:44.300 --> 00:05:46.660
and pushing them dark when they shouldn't be

00:05:46.660 --> 00:05:49.140
and taking some pixels in the dark regions

00:05:49.140 --> 00:05:50.420
and pushing them light.

00:05:50.420 --> 00:05:53.900
And that's happening random or rather proportional

00:05:53.900 --> 00:05:57.700
to how far from being light or dark each pixel is.

00:05:57.700 --> 00:06:02.020
And what that's doing is giving us, well, shades of gray.

00:06:02.020 --> 00:06:04.940
Because if you had to come up with gray scale coloring

00:06:04.940 --> 00:06:07.660
from scratch using only black and white,

00:06:07.660 --> 00:06:10.660
you'd put together like a palette of grays like this

00:06:10.660 --> 00:06:13.660
where you've got more or fewer black pixels in each one.

00:06:13.660 --> 00:06:16.540
So as you kind of lean back and blur your eyes,

00:06:16.540 --> 00:06:18.940
They become different amounts of gray.

00:06:18.940 --> 00:06:21.500
And what the random thresholds are doing

00:06:21.500 --> 00:06:23.040
is basically this,

00:06:23.040 --> 00:06:25.060
but in an ad hoc fashion.

00:06:25.060 --> 00:06:27.920
Because the brighter or darker a region is,

00:06:27.920 --> 00:06:31.540
the moral fewer pixels get pushed in the wrong direction.

00:06:31.540 --> 00:06:33.020
So we're basically hitting all these

00:06:33.020 --> 00:06:34.980
in between combinations.

00:06:34.980 --> 00:06:37.940
The only issue is random noise is, of course,

00:06:37.940 --> 00:06:39.860
doing that randomly.

00:06:39.860 --> 00:06:41.920
If only there was another way,

00:06:41.920 --> 00:06:44.020
we could do it systematically.

00:06:44.020 --> 00:06:45.220
New location,

00:06:45.220 --> 00:06:47.540
where we're gonna try a new filter,

00:06:47.540 --> 00:06:50.940
which looks like this, it's not bad.

00:06:50.940 --> 00:06:54.420
I mean my head a little bit disappears into that building,

00:06:54.420 --> 00:06:56.460
but we're keeping like, look at the detail

00:06:56.460 --> 00:06:57.820
on the hanging basket.

00:06:57.820 --> 00:06:59.980
And the background is not bad,

00:06:59.980 --> 00:07:04.380
but it does have that weird kind of old school newspaper feel

00:07:04.380 --> 00:07:07.940
and that's because it's based on a repeating pattern.

00:07:07.940 --> 00:07:10.940
Any dithering scheme that uses repeated pattern,

00:07:10.940 --> 00:07:14.020
like what we just saw, is called ordered dithering.

00:07:14.020 --> 00:07:19.020
Specifically, I was using a 2x2 Bayer-ordered dithering,

00:07:19.240 --> 00:07:21.620
which gives you five different shades of gray.

00:07:21.620 --> 00:07:23.940
Well, you get all white and all black,

00:07:23.940 --> 00:07:25.340
and then you get three in the middle

00:07:25.340 --> 00:07:29.600
where you color in one, two, or three black pixels.

00:07:29.600 --> 00:07:32.460
And that's it, that's your whole spectrum.

00:07:32.460 --> 00:07:33.540
That kinda works.

00:07:33.540 --> 00:07:36.380
You just take that, have it as a repeating tile

00:07:36.380 --> 00:07:38.500
over the entire size of your image,

00:07:38.500 --> 00:07:41.100
and then use that as your series of filters.

00:07:41.100 --> 00:07:43.420
And the way you can get it to gradually step up

00:07:43.420 --> 00:07:46.860
you just set them as different thresholds and it works.

00:07:46.860 --> 00:07:49.860
But the point is they turn on in that order.

00:07:49.860 --> 00:07:54.500
But what if we don't want just a two by two repeating pattern?

00:07:54.500 --> 00:07:56.980
Oh, well, you can go so much better.

00:07:56.980 --> 00:08:01.660
In fact, Bayer dithering works for any two to the end

00:08:01.660 --> 00:08:03.380
by two to the end grid.

00:08:03.380 --> 00:08:04.460
Allow me to demonstrate.

00:08:04.460 --> 00:08:06.660
The secret as such to Bayer dithering

00:08:06.660 --> 00:08:09.180
is the order in which the pixels flip from black to white

00:08:09.180 --> 00:08:10.580
or vice versa.

00:08:10.580 --> 00:08:13.460
And they fire off in a very specific order

00:08:13.460 --> 00:08:15.260
as an area gets brighter and brighter.

00:08:15.260 --> 00:08:16.940
The first one to go is the top left,

00:08:16.940 --> 00:08:19.620
that one there flips first, that's picks number one.

00:08:19.620 --> 00:08:22.260
As we get brighter, then number two fires,

00:08:22.260 --> 00:08:23.380
then number three,

00:08:23.380 --> 00:08:26.300
and finally we fill it in with number four.

00:08:26.300 --> 00:08:27.940
You might be thinking for a two by two grade,

00:08:27.940 --> 00:08:30.460
there's not a lot of ways to do it differently.

00:08:30.460 --> 00:08:32.780
But if you ever think about it, you could do it wrong.

00:08:32.780 --> 00:08:35.740
If you fired one and then two underneath,

00:08:35.740 --> 00:08:38.500
the moment you go up into a slightly brighter shade of gray,

00:08:38.500 --> 00:08:40.900
you get weird vertical lines appearing.

00:08:40.900 --> 00:08:42.700
And you don't want vertical horizontal lines,

00:08:42.700 --> 00:08:44.260
which is why we go diagonal

00:08:44.260 --> 00:08:46.700
and then a corner and then the other diagonal

00:08:46.700 --> 00:08:49.620
by staggering the order in which we go from white to black

00:08:49.620 --> 00:08:50.740
or black to white.

00:08:50.740 --> 00:08:52.700
You are less likely to get weird patterns.

00:08:52.700 --> 00:08:56.340
And it's better when you look at it with your human eyeballs

00:08:56.340 --> 00:08:59.180
and you kind of average it out into a gray.

00:08:59.180 --> 00:09:01.620
Things get more complicated though

00:09:01.620 --> 00:09:04.060
when we go to a bigger grid size.

00:09:04.060 --> 00:09:07.420
Bayer dithering works for any two to the end grid.

00:09:07.420 --> 00:09:10.020
So you can basically double it each time to get the next one up.

00:09:10.020 --> 00:09:13.000
And what you do is you imagine this as being

00:09:13.000 --> 00:09:15.020
like four lots of the previous ones.

00:09:15.020 --> 00:09:18.020
There's one there, another one there, another one there.

00:09:18.020 --> 00:09:21.500
And then you do the same pattern recursively

00:09:21.500 --> 00:09:22.780
as you scale it up.

00:09:22.780 --> 00:09:26.160
So of these subgrids, you always do them in that order.

00:09:26.160 --> 00:09:27.860
This one, then that one, then that one, then that one.

00:09:27.860 --> 00:09:31.260
And then within each one, you do them in this order.

00:09:31.260 --> 00:09:34.820
So for example, we're gonna do top left for all of them first

00:09:34.820 --> 00:09:35.660
in this order.

00:09:35.660 --> 00:09:42.100
we do top left there, one, top left there, two, and then three, and then four. And now

00:09:42.100 --> 00:09:49.100
we come down to here and we do them in this order. So there's five, six, seven, eight.

00:09:49.100 --> 00:09:56.260
Now we're going to do that one in all of them in that order. So that's now nine, ten,

00:09:56.260 --> 00:10:03.260
11, 12 and finally 13, 14, 15, 16.

00:10:04.620 --> 00:10:07.140
And you could repeat this and do an 8 by 8,

00:10:07.140 --> 00:10:08.860
or a 16 by 16 and so on.

00:10:08.860 --> 00:10:10.980
It's a fantastic little recursive algorithm.

00:10:10.980 --> 00:10:13.900
And if you turn the pixels on and off in this order,

00:10:13.900 --> 00:10:18.140
you get a fantastic range of shades of gray.

